"05_notify_offsets.mst — Dolphin's WM_NOTIFY handlers index past NMHDR with
 32-BIT literals.

 THE BUG, and it is the same shape as LOOSE_ENDS 3.14 one level up. That entry
 records struct field offsets baked for Win32 in a `classConstants:` block.
 These are the same numbers baked into METHOD BODIES:

     nmGetDispInfoW: pNMHDR
         ^self onDisplayDetailsRequired:
             (self itemStructure fromAddress: pNMHDR asInteger + 12)

     tvnItemExpanding: pNMHDR
         (pNMHDR uint32AtOffset: 12) == 2 ifFalse: [^0].
         tviParent := TVITEMW fromAddress: pNMHDR asInteger + 56.

 `NMHDR` is 12 bytes on Win32 and **24** on x64 — three fields, two of them
 pointer-sized, and `code` aligned after them. So every one of those literals
 points into the middle of the header instead of past it.

 WHAT IT LOOKED LIKE, which is why it survived so long: nothing raises.
 `tvnItemExpanding:` reads a word from inside `idFrom`, compares it to 2,
 finds it unequal and returns 0 — so expanding a tree node silently inserts
 nothing. `nmGetDispInfoW:` builds an item structure over the wrong address
 and fills in text and image fields that the control is not reading. The
 SCREENSHOT is what finally showed it: a ListView with a working scrollbar
 (33 rows, correct count) and not one row of text.

 THE FIX is to override the affected handlers with offsets DERIVED from the
 generated structs — `NMHDR sizeInBytes`, `NMTREEVIEWW _OffsetOf_action`,
 `NMTREEVIEWW _OffsetOf_itemNew` — which genstructs takes from winkb per
 target. A literal here would just be a 64-bit number waiting to be as wrong
 as the 32-bit one.

 NOT A STAND-IN, and so not in the scaffolding section of LOOSE_ENDS: the
 corpus's own numbers are unusable on this target and there is no version of
 this port where they become right. It is recorded as a DIVERGENCE, with the
 rule that any further handler taken from the corpus must be checked for the
 same thing.

 THE ONES NOT COVERED HERE are listed at the end, so the next person knows the
 sweep was bounded rather than complete."

"── the ?VN_GETDISPINFO family ─────────────────────────────────────────
 `NMTVDISPINFOW` and `NMLVDISPINFOW` are both `{ NMHDR hdr; <item> item; }`,
 so the item sits at exactly `NMHDR sizeInBytes` — 24 here, 12 in the corpus.
 This is what fills a tree node's or a list row's text and icon, so with it
 wrong the control draws empty rows."
Object subclass: NotifyTrace [
    <classVars: Counts>
    NotifyTrace class >> bump: aSymbol [
        Counts isNil ifTrue: [ Counts := Dictionary new ].
        Counts at: aSymbol put: (Counts at: aSymbol ifAbsent: [ 0 ]) + 1.
        ^Counts
    ]
    NotifyTrace class >> counts [ ^Counts ifNil: [ Dictionary new ] ]
    NotifyTrace class >> reset [ Counts := Dictionary new ]
]

IconicListAbstract extend [
    nmGetDispInfoW: pNMHDR [
        "Private - Handler for a ?VN_GETDISPINFO notification message.
         OVERRIDDEN: the corpus adds 12, which is the 32-bit NMHDR size."
        ^self onDisplayDetailsRequired:
            (self itemStructure fromAddress: pNMHDR asInteger + NMHDR sizeInBytes)
    ]
    nmSetDispInfoW: pNMHDR [
        "Private - Handler for a ?VN_SETDISPINFO notification message.
         OVERRIDDEN for the same reason as `nmGetDispInfoW:`."
        ^self onDisplayDetailsChanged:
            (self itemStructure fromAddress: pNMHDR asInteger + NMHDR sizeInBytes)
    ]
]

"── the tree's expand notification ─────────────────────────────────────
 `NMTREEVIEWW` is `{ NMHDR hdr; UINT action; TVITEM itemOld; TVITEM itemNew; }`.
 The corpus reads `action` at 12 and `itemNew` at 56; on this target they are
 at 24 and 88, and genstructs emits both.

 This is the one that made a tree refuse to expand: the guard on the first
 line compared a slice of `idFrom` against TVE_EXPAND and bailed out."
TreeView extend [
    tvnItemExpanding: pNMHDR [
        "Private - Default handler for the TVN_ITEMEXPANDINGW notification.
         OVERRIDDEN: 32-bit offsets in the corpus (12 and 56)."
        | tviParent hParent |
        (pNMHDR uint32AtOffset: NMTREEVIEWW _OffsetOf_action) == 2 ifFalse: [ ^0 ].
        tviParent := TVITEMW fromAddress:
            pNMHDR asInteger + NMTREEVIEWW _OffsetOf_itemNew.
        "See if we have been expanded before."
        tviParent isStateExpandedOnce ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        hParent := tviParent hItem.
        (self objectFromHandle: hParent ifAbsent: nil) ifNotNil: [ :parentObject |
            | children |
            children := self model childrenOf: parentObject.
            children isEmpty ifFalse: [
                updateMode addItems: children inHandle: hParent afterHandle: -65534 ].
            tviParent beStateExpandedOnce ].
        ^0	"Allow the expansion to proceed — TRUE would prevent it."
    ]
]

"── the custom-draw paint rect ─────────────────────────────────────────
 `NMCUSTOMDRAW` is `{ NMHDR hdr; DWORD dwDrawStage; HDC hdc; RECT rc; ... }`.
 On Win32 that puts `rc` at 20, so Dolphin tests the paint rect as
 `(pNMHDR int32AtOffset: 28) - (pNMHDR int32AtOffset: 20)` — right minus left.
 On this target `hdc` is pointer-sized and 8-aligned, so `rc` is at 40.

 Reported by `tools/audit_offsets.py` as the one literal the generic NMHDR
 rewrite cannot reach: these offsets belong to NMCUSTOMDRAW, not to the header.

 CURRENTLY HARMLESS and fixed anyway. The whole expression is guarded by
 `self customDrawBlock notNil`, and nothing in the port sets one — so it
 short-circuits before reading anything. It would have come alive silently the
 first time someone used custom draw, answering false for every item because
 the rect it measured was four fields to the left."
TreeView extend [
    wantCustomDrawItemNotifications: pNMHDR [
        "OVERRIDDEN: NMCUSTOMDRAW.rc is at 20 on Win32 and 40 here."
        | rc |
        rc := NMCUSTOMDRAW _OffsetOf_rc.
        ^self customDrawBlock notNil and: [
            (pNMHDR int32AtOffset: rc + RECT _OffsetOf_right)
                - (pNMHDR int32AtOffset: rc + RECT _OffsetOf_left) > 0
            and: [ (pNMHDR int32AtOffset: rc + RECT _OffsetOf_bottom)
                - (pNMHDR int32AtOffset: rc + RECT _OffsetOf_top) > 0 ] ]
    ]
]

"── STILL ON THE CORPUS'S 32-BIT OFFSETS ───────────────────────────────
 Bounded sweep, so the boundary is written down rather than left to be
 discovered:

   * NOTHING in `st/mvp` any more: `tools/audit_offsets.py` reports clean.
     The `+ 12` and `AtOffset: 8` families are now re-derived by
     `rewrite_nmhdr_code` in the translator, which is better than an override
     because it keeps the corpus's own logic — including the two notification
     maps that `TreeView` and `ListView` build INLINE inside `nmNotify:`.
   * `ControlView>>ttnGetDispInfoW:` builds an `NMTTDISPINFOW` `fromAddress:
     pNMHDR` with no offset at all, which is correct — the header IS the start
     of that struct — and is listed only so it is not 'fixed' by someone
     pattern-matching the others.
   * Anything reached through `nmSelChanged:` / `nmBeginDrag:` and friends
     takes the raw pNMHDR, so it is unaffected.

 THE RULE for anything new taken from the corpus: a handler that adds a
 literal to `pNMHDR`, or reads `uint32AtOffset:` from it, is wrong on this
 target until the number comes from a generated `_OffsetOf_`."

"── CCITEM>>textInBuffer: without the CRT ───────────────────────────────
 Dolphin copies an item's text into the control-supplied buffer with
 `OS.Ucrt wcsncpy_s:bufferSize:strSource:count:`. `Ucrt` is a binding this
 port has no equivalent for, so the send went to nil — and it is the LAST link
 in the ?VN_GETDISPINFO chain, the one that actually gives a tree node or a
 list row its text.

 Written directly instead, over the buffer protocol this port already has. The
 contract is exactly Dolphin's: copy at most `cchTextMax - 1` characters into
 the buffer at `pszText` and null-terminate, because the CONTROL owns that
 buffer and told us how big it is. Overrunning it corrupts comctl32's memory,
 which is why the clamp is on the control's number and not on ours.

 Ellipsis on overflow is NOT reproduced — Dolphin appends U+2026 when the text
 does not fit. Truncation is honest and visible; the ellipsis is cosmetic and
 needs the lead-surrogate test its comment describes. Recorded rather than
 silently dropped."
CCITEM extend [
    textInBuffer: anObject [
        | src dest max chars base |
        max := self cchTextMax.
        (max isNil or: [ max < 2 ]) ifTrue: [ ^anObject ].
        dest := self bytes intPtrAtOffset: self textPointerOffset.
        (dest isNil or: [ dest = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^anObject ].
        src := anObject displayString asUtf16String.
        base := src address.
        "`Utf16Buffer fromString:` allocates (n + 1) * 2 bytes, so the
         character count is half the size less the null terminator."
        chars := (src size // 2) - 1.
        chars := chars min: max - 1.
        chars < 0 ifTrue: [ chars := 0 ].
        0 to: chars * 2 - 1 do: [ :i |
            Win32 poke: dest + i put: (Win32 peek: base + i) ].
        Win32 poke: dest + (chars * 2) put: 0.
        Win32 poke: dest + (chars * 2) + 1 put: 0.
        ^anObject
    ]
]
